Chronic Sinusitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Symptoms > 12 weeks, Chronic facial pressure, Purulent post-nasal drip

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-012
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of chronic facial pressure, relentless nocturnal coughing, and severe sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed an acute exacerbation of a protracted (>12 weeks) respiratory illness, characterized by severe new-onset facial pain, persistent purulent post-nasal drip, and complete physical exhaustion due to an unremitting nocturnal cough. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to exclude acute bacterial superinfection requiring immediate high-dose antibiotic therapy, and to stabilize the severe symptom burden disrupting the patient's functional baseline prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face constantly aches, I haven't smelled anything in months, and this thick green sludge in the back of my throat makes me cough all night long."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports persistent nasal congestion and chronic obstruction lasting for the past five months. Experiences a constant dull, aching facial pressure over the maxillary and frontal paranasal sinuses. Notes a significant reduction in the sense of smell (hyposmia) and persistent halitosis. Over the last 48 hours, the thick, discolored post-nasal drip has worsened, triggering a relentless nocturnal cough that has completely prevented sleep. Previous trials of short-course oral antibiotics provided temporary relief followed by rapid relapse.
Functional Status: Independent but severely fatigued and distressed due to sleep deprivation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, missing work due to chronic illness burden.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Visibly fatigued, dark infraorbital circles, frequently clearing throat.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals edematous, hyperemic, and boggy nasal mucosa. Thick, yellow-green mucopurulent discharge is visible tracking down from the middle meatus bilaterally. Oropharyngeal exam confirms prominent, sticky post-nasal drip adhering to the posterior pharyngeal wall, which is erythematous and cobblestoned. Tenderness to firm palpation over the maxillary sinuses. Cervical lymph nodes are small, mobile, and mildly tender. Chest auscultation is clear; no adventitious sounds or prolonged expiratory phase.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty boxes of over-the-counter decongestants and cough syrups.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Has been overusing topical oxymetazoline (rebound congestion risk).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Rhinosinusitis with Acute Exacerbation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Allergic Rhinitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of thick, purulent green discharge and severe facial pain, whereas allergic rhinitis typically presents with clear, watery rhinorrhea and prominent pruritus.

Structural Nasal Obstruction (e.g., severe septal deviation/tumor) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bilateral nature of the purulent discharge and classic inflammatory mucosal changes, though underlying polyposis requires formal ENT scoping to exclude definitively.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) induced chronic cough - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct visualization of massive purulent post-nasal drip and localized facial sinus tenderness driving the cough reflex.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient suffers from an acute, severe decompensation of chronic rhinosinusitis, presenting with debilitating facial pain and sleep-preventing cough. The physical examination confirmed gross purulent discharge and mucosal inflammation, ruling out simple allergy or lower respiratory tract pathology. The chronicity of the symptoms (>12 weeks) combined with this acute purulent flare required an immediate, definitive pharmacological intervention to break the cycle of mucosal edema and infection. Delaying care until routine hours would have prolonged severe sleep deprivation and allowed further mucosal hypertrophy, increasing the risk of osteomeatal complex occlusion and localized abscess formation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a prolonged 14-day course of Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD to eradicate deep-seated bacterial load. Prescribed Intranasal Mometasone 2 sprays per nostril daily for 6 weeks to address chronic mucosal inflammation. Initiated a weaning plan for OTC topical decongestants.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically clear the stagnant mucopurulent debris, the patient must perform large-volume saline sinus irrigation twice daily. The immediate visual expulsion of thick mucus provides powerful positive reinforcement, proving the mechanical efficacy of the wash and ensuring compliance, which is absolutely critical for the topical nasal steroids to reach the target mucosa.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks for consideration of non-contrast CT Sinuses and ENT referral if symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops swelling around the eyes, double vision, severe acute headache, fever >39C, or stiff neck.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; chronic sinusitis often requires CT imaging and specialist scoping for definitive management.
Clinical Course: The acute purulence will improve over 7-10 days with antibiotics, but the underlying inflammation requires weeks of dedicated nasal steroid use.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The infection is currently contained in your sinuses. If it spreads, you will experience sudden eye swelling, changes in your vision, or a severe headache with a stiff neck. If this happens, call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Chronic sinusitis means the drainage pathways are permanently swollen shut, trapping bacteria. The long antibiotic course kills the deep bacteria, while the steroid spray slowly shrinks the swelling to open the drains.
Actionable Steps: Stop using over-the-counter decongestant sprays immediately as they cause rebound swelling. Perform the saline sinus rinse before using your prescription steroid spray to clear the path.

SMS INFORMATION

